{"cells":[{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"J8bJdtg2FPG4"},"source":["---\n","\n","# Python Programming Fundamentals for Data Enthusiasts (Cont.)\n","\n","\n","Welcome back! In this section of our Python fundamentals journey, we'll explore more advanced data structures and crucial development practices.\n","\n","This notebook will cover:\n","1.  **Advanced Collections**: Dive into `namedtuple` and `defaultdict` for more efficient and expressive data structures.\n","2.  **Debugging & IDEs**: Learn how to find and fix errors in your code, and leverage your development environment (IDE) for better productivity.\n","\n","Let's continue our learning!\n","\n","---\n","\n","## Part 3: Advanced Collections\n","\n","Python's standard `dict` (dictionary) and `tuple` are incredibly powerful. However, the `collections` module provides specialized alternatives that can be more efficient, readable, or handle specific use cases better. We'll focus on two particularly useful ones: `namedtuple` and `defaultdict`.\n","\n","---\n","\n","### 1. `namedtuple`: Readable Tuples with Field Names\n","\n","Tuples are immutable sequences, great for fixed collections of items. However, accessing items by index (e.g., `person[0]`, `person[1]`) can make code less readable, especially if the tuple has many elements and their meaning isn't immediately obvious.\n","\n","A `namedtuple` allows you to create tuple-like objects that have **fields accessible by attribute lookup** as well as by index. This makes your code much more readable and self-documenting.\n","\n","#### Basic Usage\n","\n","You define a `namedtuple` by giving it a name and a list of field names."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/","height":341},"executionInfo":{"elapsed":43,"status":"error","timestamp":1749005366875,"user":{"displayName":"James Vasanth","userId":"02578488766480362375"},"user_tz":-330},"id":"j65qeTyoFPG9","outputId":"f4ade60d-eb54-47e9-fc05-ca7dde477ef9"},"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["Point 1: Point(x=10, y=20)\n","Point 2: Point(x=5, y=15)\n","Type of p1: <class '__main__.Point'>\n","p1.x: 10\n","p1.y: 20\n","p1[0]: 10\n","p1[1]: 20\n"]},{"output_type":"error","ename":"TypeError","evalue":"'Point' object does not support item assignment","traceback":["\u001b[0;31m---------------------------------------------------------------------------\u001b[0m","\u001b[0;31mTypeError\u001b[0m                                 Traceback (most recent call last)","\u001b[0;32m<ipython-input-3-22871dcb1f3f>\u001b[0m in \u001b[0;36m<cell line: 0>\u001b[0;34m()\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1;32m     24\u001b[0m \u001b[0;31m# Namedtuples are immutable (like regular tuples)\u001b[0m\u001b[0;34m\u001b[0m\u001b[0;34m\u001b[0m\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1;32m     25\u001b[0m \u001b[0;32mtry\u001b[0m\u001b[0;34m:\u001b[0m\u001b[0;34m\u001b[0m\u001b[0;34m\u001b[0m\u001b[0m\n\u001b[0;32m---> 26\u001b[0;31m     \u001b[0mp1\u001b[0m\u001b[0;34m[\u001b[0m\u001b[0;36m0\u001b[0m\u001b[0;34m]\u001b[0m \u001b[0;34m=\u001b[0m \u001b[0;36m30\u001b[0m\u001b[0;34m\u001b[0m\u001b[0;34m\u001b[0m\u001b[0m\n\u001b[0m\u001b[1;32m     27\u001b[0m     \u001b[0mp1\u001b[0m\u001b[0;34m.\u001b[0m\u001b[0mx\u001b[0m \u001b[0;34m=\u001b[0m \u001b[0;36m30\u001b[0m \u001b[0;31m# This will raise an AttributeError\u001b[0m\u001b[0;34m\u001b[0m\u001b[0;34m\u001b[0m\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1;32m     28\u001b[0m \u001b[0;32mexcept\u001b[0m \u001b[0mAttributeError\u001b[0m \u001b[0;32mas\u001b[0m \u001b[0me\u001b[0m\u001b[0;34m:\u001b[0m\u001b[0;34m\u001b[0m\u001b[0;34m\u001b[0m\u001b[0m\n","\u001b[0;31mTypeError\u001b[0m: 'Point' object does not support item assignment"]}],"source":["from collections import namedtuple\n","\n","# Define a namedtuple for a Point\n","# 'Point' is the name of the new namedtuple class\n","# ['x', 'y'] are the field names\n","Point = namedtuple('Point', ['x', 'y'])\n","\n","# Create instances of Point\n","p1 = Point(10, 20)\n","p2 = Point(x=5, y=15) # You can also use keyword arguments\n","\n","print(f\"Point 1: {p1}\")\n","print(f\"Point 2: {p2}\")\n","print(f\"Type of p1: {type(p1)}\")\n","\n","# Access elements by name (more readable!)\n","print(f\"p1.x: {p1.x}\")\n","print(f\"p1.y: {p1.y}\")\n","\n","# You can still access by index (since it's a tuple)\n","print(f\"p1[0]: {p1[0]}\")\n","print(f\"p1[1]: {p1[1]}\")\n","\n","# Namedtuples are immutable (like regular tuples)\n","try:\n","    p1[0] = 30\n","    p1.x = 30 # This will raise an AttributeError\n","except AttributeError as e:\n","    print(f\"\\nError: {e}. Namedtuples are immutable!\")"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"acg_De0XFPG_"},"source":["#### Real-world Example: Representing a Student Record\n","\n","Imagine you're dealing with student data. A regular tuple might look like `('Alice', 20, 'Computer Science', 3.8)`. Using a `namedtuple` makes it much clearer."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"executionInfo":{"elapsed":44,"status":"ok","timestamp":1749005820858,"user":{"displayName":"James Vasanth","userId":"02578488766480362375"},"user_tz":-330},"id":"H9zGE-H8FPG_","outputId":"28b77dcc-462a-4acb-d6b2-046510fc888e"},"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["\n","Student 1: Student(name='Alice', age=20, major='Computer Science', gpa=3.8)\n","Student 2: Student(name='Bob', age=22, major='Physics', gpa=3.5)\n","\n","Alice is 20 years old and majors in Computer Science.\n","Bob's GPA: 3.5\n","Student 1 as dictionary: {'name': 'Alice', 'age': 20, 'major': 'Computer Science', 'gpa': 3.8}\n","\n","Iterating over s1:\n","Alice\n","20\n","Computer Science\n","3.8\n","\n","Iterating over s1:\n","0 Alice\n","1 20\n","2 Computer Science\n","3 3.8\n"]}],"source":["from collections import namedtuple\n","\n","# Define a namedtuple for Student\n","Student = namedtuple('Student', ['name', 'age', 'major', 'gpa'])\n","\n","# Create student records\n","s1 = Student('Alice', 20, 'Computer Science', 3.8)\n","s2 = Student(name='Bob', age=22,  gpa=3.5, major='Physics')\n","\n","print(f\"\\nStudent 1: {s1}\")\n","print(f\"Student 2: {s2}\")\n","\n","# Accessing data is more intuitive\n","print(f\"\\n{s1.name} is {s1.age} years old and majors in {s1.major}.\")\n","print(f\"Bob's GPA: {s2.gpa}\")\n","\n","# Convert to dictionary (useful for serialization or other operations)\n","print(f\"Student 1 as dictionary: {s1._asdict()}\")\n","\n","# Iterate over a namedtuple\n","print(\"\\nIterating over s1:\")\n","for field_value in s1:\n","    print(field_value)\n","\n","# Iterate over a namedtuple\n","print(\"\\nIterating over s1:\")\n","for field_value in range(len(s1)):\n","    print(field_value, s1[field_value])"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"10yTYx0zFPG_"},"source":["#### When to use `namedtuple`:\n","\n","* When you need an **immutable** object with a fixed set of fields.\n","* When you want to improve the **readability** of your code by giving names to tuple elements instead of using numerical indices.\n","* As lightweight data structures, especially when returning multiple values from a function or passing simple records around.\n","\n","---\n","\n","### 2. `defaultdict`: Dictionaries with Default Values\n","\n","Regular dictionaries raise a `KeyError` if you try to access a key that doesn't exist. This often forces you to write boilerplate code to check for a key's existence before trying to update its value, especially when building counts or grouping items.\n","\n","A `defaultdict` from the `collections` module is a subclass of `dict` that provides a default value for a key that does not exist. You provide a **factory function** (like `int`, `list`, `set`, etc.) when you create the `defaultdict`. When you try to access a non-existent key, the `defaultdict` calls this factory function to create a default value for that key and inserts it into the dictionary before returning it.\n","\n","#### Basic Usage"]},{"cell_type":"code","source":["fruits = {}\n","print(fruits['apple'])"],"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/","height":162},"id":"yRrpVWFb8_nJ","executionInfo":{"status":"error","timestamp":1749007227511,"user_tz":-330,"elapsed":42,"user":{"displayName":"James Vasanth","userId":"02578488766480362375"}},"outputId":"b843dd8a-575c-406f-e6f0-c16bcfd4315d"},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[{"output_type":"error","ename":"KeyError","evalue":"'apple'","traceback":["\u001b[0;31m---------------------------------------------------------------------------\u001b[0m","\u001b[0;31mKeyError\u001b[0m                                  Traceback (most recent call last)","\u001b[0;32m<ipython-input-12-c6cc8ccf6a6a>\u001b[0m in \u001b[0;36m<cell line: 0>\u001b[0;34m()\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1;32m      1\u001b[0m \u001b[0mfruits\u001b[0m \u001b[0;34m=\u001b[0m \u001b[0;34m{\u001b[0m\u001b[0;34m}\u001b[0m\u001b[0;34m\u001b[0m\u001b[0;34m\u001b[0m\u001b[0m\n\u001b[0;32m----> 2\u001b[0;31m \u001b[0mprint\u001b[0m\u001b[0;34m(\u001b[0m\u001b[0mfruits\u001b[0m\u001b[0;34m[\u001b[0m\u001b[0;34m'apple'\u001b[0m\u001b[0;34m]\u001b[0m\u001b[0;34m)\u001b[0m\u001b[0;34m\u001b[0m\u001b[0;34m\u001b[0m\u001b[0m\n\u001b[0m","\u001b[0;31mKeyError\u001b[0m: 'apple'"]}]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"executionInfo":{"elapsed":7,"status":"ok","timestamp":1753415435262,"user":{"displayName":"James Vasanth","userId":"02578488766480362375"},"user_tz":-330},"id":"cx89rxskFPHA","outputId":"deb6caec-f2a8-4f2a-d98b-42f94381fa95"},"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["--- Counting words with defaultdict(int) ---\n","defaultdict(<class 'int'>, {})\n","Value for the item- apple 0\n","After processing 'apple': {'apple': 1}\n","Value for the item- banana 0\n","After processing 'banana': {'apple': 1, 'banana': 1}\n","Value for the item- apple 1\n","After processing 'apple': {'apple': 2, 'banana': 1}\n","Value for the item- orange 0\n","After processing 'orange': {'apple': 2, 'banana': 1, 'orange': 1}\n","Value for the item- banana 1\n","After processing 'banana': {'apple': 2, 'banana': 2, 'orange': 1}\n","Value for the item- apple 2\n","After processing 'apple': {'apple': 3, 'banana': 2, 'orange': 1}\n","\n","Final word counts: {'apple': 3, 'banana': 2, 'orange': 1}\n"]}],"source":["from collections import defaultdict\n","\n","# Create a defaultdict with int as the default factory\n","# If a key is accessed that doesn't exist, it defaults to 0 (int())\n","\n","word_string = defaultdict(str)\n","\n","words = [\"apple\", \"banana\", \"apple\", \"orange\", \"banana\", \"apple\"]\n","word_counts = defaultdict(int)\n","print(\"--- Counting words with defaultdict(int) ---\")\n","print(word_counts)\n","for word in words:\n","    # word_string[word]\n","    # print(f\"After processing '{word}': {dict(word_string)}\")\n","    # break\n","    print(f\"Value for the item - {word}\", word_counts[word])\n","    word_counts[word] += 1 # If 'word' doesn't exist, it starts at 0, then increments\n","    print(f\"After processing '{word}': {dict(word_counts)}\") # Convert to dict for clear print\n","\n","print(f\"\\nFinal word counts: {dict(word_counts)}\") # Convert back to regular dict for final print"]},{"cell_type":"code","source":["from collections import defaultdict\n","\n","# Create a defaultdict with int as the default factory\n","# If a key is accessed that doesn't exist, it defaults to 0 (int())\n","\n","word_string = defaultdict(str)\n","words = [\"apple\", \"banana\", \"apple\", \"orange\", \"banana\", \"apple\"]\n","print(word_string)\n","i=0\n","for word in words:\n","    word_string[word] = word + \"-\" + str(i)\n","    print(f\"After processing '{word}': {dict(word_string)}\")\n","    i += 1\n","    # break\n","\n","\n","print(f\"\\nFinal word counts: {dict(word_counts)}\") # Convert back to regular dict for final print"],"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"id":"wAQLjz4wtOE7","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1753415669735,"user_tz":-330,"elapsed":9,"user":{"displayName":"James Vasanth","userId":"02578488766480362375"}},"outputId":"842af49e-11a5-4b61-866a-7bbea8e832bf"},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["defaultdict(<class 'str'>, {})\n","After processing 'apple': {'apple': 'apple-0'}\n","After processing 'banana': {'apple': 'apple-0', 'banana': 'banana-1'}\n","After processing 'apple': {'apple': 'apple-2', 'banana': 'banana-1'}\n","After processing 'orange': {'apple': 'apple-2', 'banana': 'banana-1', 'orange': 'orange-3'}\n","After processing 'banana': {'apple': 'apple-2', 'banana': 'banana-4', 'orange': 'orange-3'}\n","After processing 'apple': {'apple': 'apple-5', 'banana': 'banana-4', 'orange': 'orange-3'}\n","\n","Final word counts: {'apple': 3, 'banana': 2, 'orange': 1}\n"]}]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"qW-D507LFPHA"},"source":["#### Real-world Example: Grouping Data\n","\n","This is extremely useful for grouping items into lists or sets."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"executionInfo":{"elapsed":13,"status":"ok","timestamp":1753416083126,"user":{"displayName":"James Vasanth","userId":"02578488766480362375"},"user_tz":-330},"id":"A2ksYdTeFPHB","outputId":"8eed76e6-1d82-4c8f-b297-0fe99b25977d"},"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["\n","--- Grouping items with defaultdict(list) ---\n","After processing (fruit, apple): {'fruit': ['apple']}\n","After processing (vegetable, carrot): {'fruit': ['apple'], 'vegetable': ['carrot']}\n","After processing (fruit, banana): {'fruit': ['apple', 'banana'], 'vegetable': ['carrot']}\n","After processing (meat, chicken): {'fruit': ['apple', 'banana'], 'vegetable': ['carrot'], 'meat': ['chicken']}\n","After processing (vegetable, spinach): {'fruit': ['apple', 'banana'], 'vegetable': ['carrot', 'spinach'], 'meat': ['chicken']}\n","After processing (fruit, orange): {'fruit': ['apple', 'banana', 'orange'], 'vegetable': ['carrot', 'spinach'], 'meat': ['chicken']}\n","After processing (fruit, apple): {'fruit': ['apple', 'banana', 'orange', 'apple'], 'vegetable': ['carrot', 'spinach'], 'meat': ['chicken']}\n","\n","Final grouped items: {'fruit': ['apple', 'banana', 'orange', 'apple'], 'vegetable': ['carrot', 'spinach'], 'meat': ['chicken']}\n","\n","--- Grouping unique items with defaultdict(set) ---\n","After processing (color, red): {'color': {'red'}}\n","After processing (shape, circle): {'color': {'red'}, 'shape': {'circle'}}\n","After processing (color, blue): {'color': {'red', 'blue'}, 'shape': {'circle'}}\n","After processing (color, red): {'color': {'red', 'blue'}, 'shape': {'circle'}}\n","After processing (shape, square): {'color': {'red', 'blue'}, 'shape': {'square', 'circle'}}\n","\n","Final grouped unique items: {'color': {'red', 'blue'}, 'shape': {'square', 'circle'}}\n"]}],"source":["from collections import defaultdict\n","\n","data_points = [\n","    ('fruit', 'apple'),\n","    ('vegetable', 'carrot'),\n","    ('fruit', 'banana'),\n","    ('meat', 'chicken'),\n","    ('vegetable', 'spinach'),\n","    ('fruit', 'orange'),\n","    ('fruit', 'apple'),\n","]\n","\n","# Create a defaultdict with list as the default factory\n","# If a key doesn't exist, it defaults to an empty list ([])\n","grouped_items = defaultdict(list)\n","\n","print(\"\\n--- Grouping items with defaultdict(list) ---\")\n","for category, item in data_points:\n","    grouped_items[category].append(item) # If 'category' doesn't exist, it starts as [], then appends\n","    print(f\"After processing ({category}, {item}): {dict(grouped_items)}\")\n","\n","print(f\"\\nFinal grouped items: {dict(grouped_items)}\")\n","\n","# Example with sets to avoid duplicates\n","grouped_unique_items = defaultdict(set)\n","data_for_set = [\n","    ('color', 'red'),\n","    ('shape', 'circle'),\n","     ('color', 'blue'),\n","    ('color', 'red'),\n","     ('shape', 'square')\n","]\n","\n","print(\"\\n--- Grouping unique items with defaultdict(set) ---\")\n","for category, item in data_for_set:\n","    grouped_unique_items[category].add(item)\n","    print(f\"After processing ({category}, {item}): {dict(grouped_unique_items)}\")\n","\n","print(f\"\\nFinal grouped unique items: {dict(grouped_unique_items)}\")"]},{"cell_type":"code","source":["data_points = [\n","    ('fruit', 'apple'),\n","    ('vegetable', 'carrot'),\n","    ('fruit', 'banana'),\n","    ('meat', 'chicken'),\n","    ('vegetable', 'spinach'),\n","    ('fruit', 'orange')\n","]\n","\n","grouped_data = {}\n","for key, value in data_points:\n","    if key not in grouped_data:\n","        grouped_data[key] = []\n","    grouped_data[key].append(value)\n","\n","print(grouped_data)\n","# Expected output: {'fruit': ['apple', 'banana', 'orange'], 'vegetable': ['carrot', 'spinach'], 'meat': ['chicken']}"],"metadata":{"id":"SvihgVps_-xP","colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1753416178792,"user_tz":-330,"elapsed":15,"user":{"displayName":"James Vasanth","userId":"02578488766480362375"}},"outputId":"3e3f3fc4-57c8-4221-9831-0a54f1973013"},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["{'fruit': ['apple', 'banana', 'orange'], 'vegetable': ['carrot', 'spinach'], 'meat': ['chicken']}\n"]}]},{"cell_type":"code","source":["from collections import defaultdict\n","\n","data_points = [\n","    ('fruit', 'apple'),\n","    ('vegetable', 'carrot'),\n","    ('fruit', 'banana'),\n","    ('meat', 'chicken'),\n","    ('vegetable', 'spinach'),\n","    ('fruit', 'orange')\n","]\n","\n","\n","grouped_items = defaultdict(list)\n","for category, item in data_points:\n","    grouped_items[category].append(item)\n"],"metadata":{"id":"Rndv3w4mAERO"},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"rQGANMWAFPHB"},"source":["#### When to use `defaultdict`:\n","\n","* When you need to **count** occurrences of items.\n","* When you need to **group** items into lists, sets, or other collections based on a key.\n","* To simplify code that would otherwise require explicit `if key in dict:` checks.\n","\n","---\n","\n","## Part 4: Debugging and Breakpoints, Using IDEs\n","\n","Writing code is just one part of programming. A significant portion of a developer's time is spent finding and fixing errors – a process called **debugging**. An **Integrated Development Environment (IDE)** is a software application that provides comprehensive facilities to computer programmers for software development. Jupyter Notebook, while not a full-fledged IDE, has some basic debugging capabilities, and understanding IDE features is crucial.\n","\n","---\n","\n","### 1. What is Debugging?\n","\n","**Debugging** is the process of identifying, analyzing, and removing errors (bugs) from computer programs. When your code doesn't work as expected, debugging helps you understand *why* by letting you examine your program's state (variable values, execution flow) step-by-step.\n","\n","#### Common Ways to Debug:\n","\n","1.  **Print Statements:** The simplest (and often first) method. Sprinkle `print()` statements throughout your code to display variable values and check if certain parts of your code are being executed."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"id":"mgOVCQwpFPHC","colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1753416532486,"user_tz":-330,"elapsed":22,"user":{"displayName":"James Vasanth","userId":"02578488766480362375"}},"outputId":"e6e5dd12-44c8-4ed9-f52d-fce80bf6d50f"},"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["DEBUG: Input numbers: [10, 20, 30]\n","DEBUG: Total sum: 60\n","DEBUG: Calculated average: 20.0\n","20.0\n","DEBUG: Input numbers: []\n","DEBUG: Numbers list is empty, returning 0.\n","0\n"]}],"source":["def calculate_average(numbers):\n","        print(f\"DEBUG: Input numbers: {numbers}\") # Debug print\n","        if not numbers:\n","            print(\"DEBUG: Numbers list is empty, returning 0.\") # Debug print\n","            return 0\n","        total = sum(numbers)\n","        print(f\"DEBUG: Total sum: {total}\") # Debug print\n","        count = len(numbers)\n","        average = total / count\n","        print(f\"DEBUG: Calculated average: {average}\") # Debug print\n","        return average\n","\n","print(calculate_average([10, 20, 30]))\n","print(calculate_average([]))"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"YNwzHLUeFPHC"},"source":["**Pros:** Easy to use, no special tools needed.\n","    **Cons:** Can clutter code, difficult for complex logic, you have to remove them later.\n","\n","2.  **Using a Debugger (with Breakpoints):** This is the professional and most efficient way to debug. A debugger is a tool that allows you to:\n","    * **Set Breakpoints:** Pause program execution at specific lines of code.\n","    * **Step Through Code:** Execute code line by line.\n","    * **Inspect Variables:** View the current values of all variables in scope.\n","    * **Call Stack:** See the sequence of function calls that led to the current point.\n","\n","---\n","\n","### 2. Debugging in Jupyter Notebooks (Basic)\n","\n","Jupyter Notebooks have a built-in magic command `%debug` that can help you with basic debugging when an error occurs. For more structured debugging, you often use the `pdb` module or external IDEs.\n","\n","#### Using `%debug` after an error:\n","\n","When a cell produces an unhandled exception (an error), you can run `%debug` in a new cell immediately afterward to enter the Python debugger (PDB)."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/","height":305},"executionInfo":{"elapsed":84,"status":"error","timestamp":1749091677203,"user":{"displayName":"James Vasanth","userId":"02578488766480362375"},"user